course: dictionary
lesson: 15917
title: Unpersuasive arguments
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To persuade someone is to make them believe something or agree with you. Persuasive means good at doing that. Add «un-» and you get unpersuasive, which means not good at making people believe or agree. The prefix un- means not, so unpersuasive is the opposite of persuasive.
explain: «I should get a new bike because I want one.» This is unpersuasive. Wanting something is not a reason that makes anyone agree. Ask yourself: did the writer give strong reasons or evidence? If not, the argument is unpersuasive.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «not convincing» in place of unpersuasive: «The argument was not convincing» still makes sense, so that is its meaning. In English lessons, persuasive writing and debates need good reasons, so a weak argument is unpersuasive.
fact: Unpersuasive means not convincing.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so unpersuasive is the opposite of persuasive.
fact: A persuasive argument uses strong reasons and evidence to make people agree.
fact: «The excuse was unpersuasive, so Mum did not believe me.» Here the excuse did not work.
fact: An argument can be unpersuasive in a debate, in a persuasive letter, or in an everyday excuse.
exercise: What does unpersuasive mean? | Very loud and cheerful | *Not convincing | Long and full of pictures | Easy to read
exercise: Which word means the opposite of persuasive? | gentle | loud | happy | *unpersuasive
exercise: Which argument is unpersuasive? | «I have read three books about dogs and made a plan for feeding it.» | «I will walk it every day, and I have saved money for its food.» | *«I want a puppy because I want one.» | «Puppies need care, vets, and time, and my family has agreed to share the work.»
test: Ella's excuse was unpersuasive. What does that tell us? | *Her teacher was not convinced | Her teacher laughed at her joke | Her teacher gave her a prize | Her teacher was asleep
test: What makes an argument persuasive? | Nothing at all, because all arguments are the same | Loud voices | Long words, even if they are silly | *Strong reasons and evidence
exam: Which sentence shows an unpersuasive argument? | «Reading at lunch helps us relax, and the library is quiet.» | *«Everyone should have extra time at break because it is my birthday.» | «Breaks help us rest, and a longer break gives us more time to play.» | «Our school has a garden, and we could look after it with a rota.»
